<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alesia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alesia was the capital of the Mandubii, a Gallic tribe allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aedui">Aedui</a>, on Mont-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxois">Auxois</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alise-sainte-reine">Alise-Sainte-Reine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The Celtic oppidum stood on a plateau of about 97 hectares, some 200 metres above the valley floor. In 52 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> there: in six weeks his troops built a ring of fortifications 15 km long around the town and another 21 km long to hold off relief, and the Roman victory decided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-wars">Gallic Wars</a> and the fate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a>. Alesia then became a Gallo-Roman town with temples, a forum and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>. Its long-disputed site was confirmed by excavations in 1861–1865 and again in 1991–97.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alesia-city.jpg" alt="Alesia" width="330" height="284" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Epona21 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_aérienne_vestiges_Alésia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 58 and 50 BCE Julius Caesar conquered the rest of Gaul; Vercingetorix of the Arverni united most Gauls against him in 52 BCE and held Gergovia, but surrendered at <b>Alesia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/celts">Celts · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>In 52 BCE they sent ten thousand men to <b>Alesia</b>, and their leader Sedullus was killed there.</blockquote><a href="/state/lemovices">Lemovices</a></li><li><blockquote>After the surrender <b>Alesia</b> lived on as a Gallo-Roman town.</blockquote><a href="/state/mandubii">Mandubii</a></li></ul></section>
